  "text": "---\nseo_options:\n  seo_title: Manga Reads Insomniacs After School Review and Recommendations\n  seo_description: Ganta Nakami and Isaki Magari are two insomniacs who, through serendipitous fate, end up meeting in their school's inactive observatory.\ntags: [manga, review, recommendations, Insomniacs After School, high school, slice-of-life, romance, drama, astronomy, photography astrophotography, Love at Fourteed, Orange]\ntopics: [Manga, Book Review, Book Recommendations, Japanese Culture, Anime]\n---\n\n# Review: Insomniacs After School\n\n### A Picture is Worth a Thousand Words\n\nI was very excited to find this manga because I happen to like astronomy. What I ended up finding hit a lot closer to home than I ever would have imagined. I even hesitated writing this because I can personally relate to so many of the themes in the book, everything from astronomy and astrophotography to the main character's (Nakami) struggles.\n\n![Insomniacs After School manga cover 1](../../../images/insomniacs_after_school_cover_1.jpg)\n\nPerhaps that is why the following review may seem more critical of the faults of the series than I normally write about. I had high hopes for this series and for the most part it didn't let me down, but given how empathetic I am to the characters and setting I have probably thought more about this series than others.\n\n## Story\n\nWhile the title is certainly descriptive of the main characters, two insomniacs, Ganta Nakami and Isaki Magari, the astronomy club setting is a little bit misleading. I was expecting the series to take place primarily in the depicted observatory from the cover but the most prominent theme of astronomy is actually found in the form of outdoor astrophotography which becomes a motivating factor through the first two-thirds of the series while high school life, romance, and drama dominate the final third. The story begins with the two protagonists serendipitously meeting in their school's inactive observatory where Nakami stumbles upon Magari sleeping in a box.\n\nThe story reveals that both protagonists suffer from insomnia, leading them to seek isolation during the day to catch up on their sleep. Nakami is described as smart but anti-social, and because he is always tired, he has little energy to interact with others. Magari, on the other hand, is a fairly sociable girl with many friends. In a somewhat contrived turn of events, Nakami and Magari begin to make the observatory their own private hideout by slowly pulling in furnishings and other odds and ends to make the room more comfortable. The two even go out for a late-night rendezvous in a display of teenage \"rebellion,\" fully aware that they should not be out at night and could be caught and reported. Their luck doesn't last, though, as the school nurse, Kurashiki-sensei, catches them in the observatory with a completely expected question: \"You two aren't up to any funny business in here, are you?\" This foreshadows the expected relationship that blossoms between the two.\n\nWhat follows is the first of many Kurashiki-sensei interventions, in which she convinces the school principal to make Nakami and Magari formal members of the previously defunct Astronomy Club, where they are expected to begin club activities. This leads to the introduction of the last former member of the Astronomy Club, Yui Shiromaru, and Nakami's exposure to astrophotography. The series follows the Astronomy Club's efforts to submit an award-winning photo as evidence of actual club activities so that it can be officially recognized by the school. During this time, Nakami and Magari, the club's only two members, begin to open up to one another as they plan school participation, meteor-shower viewing parties, and trips in a somewhat larger-than-life fairy-tale way. The climax of the first third of the series comes after the two finish their club trip to take their photo for the contest, which segues the characters into the second arc of the story which focuses more on club activities and Nakami and Magari's relationship. That said, I won't go into details about the latter arcs of the story here to prevent writing any spoilers. Just know that there are a couple of jarring time jumps that skip over some potentially great plot points that could have provided opportunities for the characters to show their growth and development which leads to some of my further commentary regarding the story.\n\nOne of my criticisms of the story is its avoidance of character development in new or different situations the characters encounter; the mangaka chooses to avoid these scenes by simply skipping ahead in time. Sometimes this can mean choosing not to follow through with a specific plot point and moving on to the next chronological scene, but there are other times when the skips are larger and more jarring to the reader. For instance, in the final third of the story, Nakami and Magari enter their second year of high school and the better part of volume 11 is spent with the two focused on recruiting new members to the Astronomy Club. However, when they finally make it to the point where three new members have joined, the story jumps ahead over a year in volume 12 to Nakami and Magari as third years. The story does not show them interacting with the new club members, preparing for activities, or getting to know and understand the new students. Additionally, Nakami and Magari's relationship appears exactly the same after the time jump as before without any changes after a year of dating. I think this is a direct result of how the story is presented, primarily through the artwork, setting, panels, sequencing, body language, and facial expressions, which are all used to convey the emotions of the story to the reader indirectly, without dialogue. This is one of the greatest strengths of this series, but also its greatest failing. This particular style of storytelling leads to an unfortunate lack of character growth for Nakami and Magari who show the same faults at the end of the series as they had at the beginning.\n\nI'd also like to take a moment to call out the true hero and deus ex machina of this story, Kurashiki-sensei, an underappreciated and overlooked character. She is the catalyst who provides resolution, or at least direction, to every major roadblock the protagonists encounter. The story starts with her convincing the principal to make Nakami and Magari members of the inactive Astronomy Club. She points them in the direction of the former member Shiromaru to learn about club activities and get them involved in astrophotography. Even at the end of the story, when Nakami is stuck in his same character fault, it is Kurashiki-sensei who pushes him to move forward. Make no mistake: this story is about Nakami and Magari, but none of it would have happened without Kurashiki-sensei. Another issue is that the story introduces numerous side characters, many of whom are left with incomplete stories. This is perhaps another result of the primarily visual narrative, but leaves the reader feeling like their stories are left without closure.\n\n## Style\n\nOne of the absolute highlights of this series is the artwork. It's a stunning visual display that is able to evoke feelings from the reader even without the aid of dialogue. In fact, there are often several pages at a time where there is hardly a word spoken between Nakami and Magari, but their expressions, body language, and backgrounds are enough to move the reader. The dialogue is relatively weak in many parts of the series, deferring to visual narrative instead, but when there is dialogue, it does well to move the story along and fill in the details of the story. Often, series rely on dialogue to convey the story, with the artwork as support; _Insomniacs After School_ does the opposite, using text to fill in the gaps.\n\nGiven the prominence of astrophotography and photography in general throughout the series, it comes as no surprise that the backgrounds, settings, and locations are extraordinarily detailed. The night scenes are works of art, and the heavy usage of 2-page spreads fits well, allowing the mangaka to display stunning scenes for the reader to take in and appreciate. Even the day-to-day locations at school or in the observatory display thorough details, truly making the panels feel like a picture is worth a thousand words.\n\nThe characters themselves are unique in their styling and fit the theme well. Nakami and Magari aren't generic-looking, but they also aren't extraordinarily detailed in their design. I'd say their most unique styling is their clothing, especially Magari's, which often stands out for being very detailed. Nakami and Magari are often seen in settings outside of school, which allows a greater degree of freedom for them to be shown in different outfits that fit their location, whether they're walking through a festival or camping out at night.\n\n## Recommendations\n\n_Insomniacs After School_ is certainly a series worthy of praise, but it isn't without its shortcomings. Its sometimes incongruent storytelling or avoidance of character development can leave the reader feeling empty, as if the story has missed a critical plot point. If you're looking for some other titles that might fill in some of the pieces you feel are missing from _Insomniacs After School_, check out _Love at Fourteen_. The characters are just a tad younger than Nakami and Magari, but the school romance is full of dialogue that draws the reader in, perhaps even making them a bit nostalgic for those youthful days. On the other hand, if the drama and melancholy are what you're seeking, take a look at _Orange_. It, too, is a high school romance with a science-fiction twist, but readers, be warned, _Orange_ features tragedy as a major driving force in the story. When all is said and done, I absolutely recommend _Insomniacs After School_ to readers. _Insomniacs After School_ brings together the emotional story of _Love at Fourteen_ and the drama of _Orange_ into a strong visual narrative with beautiful scenes that pull the reader into the story.\n\n![ manga cover 1](../../../images/love_at_fourteen_cover_1.png)\n![ manga cover 1](../../../images/orange_cover_1.jpg)\n",
